The first step in the process is to contact a health care provider to obtain a referral to a medical specialist with experience in the diagnostic evaluation of exposure to environmental chemicals and toxins. The medical specialist will evaluate the patient's health concerns, exposure concerns, and test results in the context of established standards of care and recommend further testing, if appropriate. The patient should attempt to gather as much information as possible about their exposures before seeing the specialist. This fact sheet provides guidance about how to gather exposure information and the types of tests that can be performed.


Exposure Assessment

The first step in determining whether you or your child has been exposed to toxic substances is to gather as much information as possible about your or your child's past and present activities. It is important to try to remember as many details as possible about when and where you think your child may have been exposed to toxic substances, including:

  • Places where you live, work, go to school, or spend time;
  • Family and friends' homes and yards;
  • Specific locations where hazardous materials may have been released, e.g., a chemical spill;
  • Specific times, including dates, when and how the activities occurred;
  • Who were with you and your child when the activities occurred; and
  • What activities were being done.

Different tests are available to determine if specific chemicals or toxicants are present in the body. These tests can be helpful if you think your child may have been exposed to a particular chemical, such as lead or mercury. Some of the chemicals for which testing is available include:

  • Airborne toxins, e.g., biotoxins;
  • Metals, e.g., lead, mercury, arsenic;
  • Pesticides, e.g., organophosphate pesticides, chlorpyrifos, diazinon, malathion;
  • Solvents, e.g., trichloroethylene, tetrachlorethylene;
  • Dry cleaning fluids;
  • PCBs and other industrial chemicals;
  • PBA plastic;
  • Mold and mycotoxins;
  • Drugs of abuse;
  • Toxic smoke and ash;
  • Radon; and
  • Other chemicals, e.g., Bisphenol A (BPA), phthalates.

